  "solutions": [
    {
      "id": "sol.amc-12b-2011-p7.live",
      "kind": "proof-sketch",
      "text": "Since \\(\\dfrac{x+y}{2}=60,\\) we have \\(x+y=120.\\) To maximize \\(\\dfrac{x}{y}\\) we make \\(y\\) small.\n\nBecause \\(x\\le99,\\) it follows that \\(y=120-x\\ge21.\\) Taking \\(x=99\\) and \\(y=21\\) gives the maximum \\[ \\dfrac{99}{21}=\\dfrac{33}{7}. \\]\n\nThus, the correct answer is B.",
      "hints": [
        "The mean condition gives \\(x+y=120\\)",
        "To maximize \\(\\dfrac{x}{y},\\) make \\(x\\) as large and \\(y\\) as small as allowed"
      ],
